---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ArticleFeedback from "../../components/ArticleFeedback.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
    title="How to Take Natural Photos of Yourself When Nobody Is Behind the Camera"
    description="How to take natural photos of yourself when nobody is behind the camera, using timers, video, remotes, and simple activities."
>
    <main class="article-page">
        <article>
            <header class="article-header">
                <p class="eyebrow">PHOTOS &amp; CAMERA CONFIDENCE</p>

                <h1>
                    How to Take Natural Photos of Yourself When Nobody Is Behind
                    the Camera
                </h1>

                <p class="article-intro">
                    Taking a natural photo of yourself sounds simple until you
                    actually try it.
                </p>
            </header>

            <div class="article-content">
                <p>
                    You put your phone somewhere. Set the timer. Walk into
                    place.
                </p>

                <p>Then you wait.</p>

                <p>
                    And suddenly you are very aware that you are standing in
                    front of a phone that is about to take your picture.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You start thinking about your face. Your hands. Whether you
                    are standing normally. Whether you should smile.
                </p>

                <p>The timer counts down.</p>

                <p>You take the photo.</p>

                <p>
                    And somehow you look like someone who knew a photo was being
                    taken.
                </p>

                <p>Which, of course, you did.</p>

                <p>
                    That is the slightly annoying part of taking your own
                    photos. You are not only the person being photographed. You
                    are also the person controlling the camera.
                </p>

                <p>
                    There is no one else to create a little distance between you
                    and the photograph.
                </p>

                <h2>The problem isn’t necessarily the timer</h2>

                <p>A timer can be useful. So can a remote shutter.</p>

                <p>But neither one solves the whole problem.</p>

                <p>
                    The real difficulty is that you know exactly when the
                    photograph is happening.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If you press the button and then stand still waiting for the
                    picture, your attention naturally turns toward the camera.
                </p>

                <p>Now the camera is the activity.</p>

                <p>
                    And that makes it surprisingly difficult to look like you
                    were doing something else.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This is why simply being told to “act natural” usually
                    doesn’t help much.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You cannot really relax while checking whether you look
                    relaxed.
                </p>

                <h2>Give the camera less of your attention</h2>

                <p>
                    One way to make taking photos alone easier is to stop
                    treating the photograph as the main event.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Instead of walking into position and waiting for the camera,
                    give yourself something to do.
                </p>

                <p>Make coffee.</p>

                <p>Walk across the room.</p>

                <p>Look through a bookshelf.</p>

                <p>Water a plant.</p>

                <p>Pick something up.</p>

                <p>Adjust your bag before leaving the house.</p>

                <p>
                    The activity doesn’t need to be interesting enough for a
                    movie.
                </p>

                <p>It just needs to give your attention somewhere to go.</p>

                <p>This changes something small but important.</p>

                <p>You are no longer trying to create a natural expression.</p>

                <p>
                    You are doing something that naturally creates an
                    expression.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Your hands have a reason to move. Your body has somewhere to
                    go. Your face has something to react to.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The photograph becomes a record of the activity instead of
                    the activity itself.
                </p>

                <h2>This is where video can help</h2>

                <p>
                    Sometimes even a timer leaves too much pressure on a single
                    moment.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You have ten seconds to get into position, and then you have
                    one frame.
                </p>

                <p>If you don’t like it, you do it again.</p>

                <p>And again.</p>

                <p>
                    Eventually, you are not doing the activity anymore. You are
                    doing repeated versions of the activity for the camera.
                </p>

                <p>Video can change that.</p>

                <p>
                    Instead of asking yourself to find one perfect moment, you
                    can let several moments happen and choose a frame afterward.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You might walk toward the camera, turn around, pick up your
                    coffee, sit down, or simply move through an ordinary part of
                    your day.
                </p>

                <p>The camera records the whole thing.</p>

                <p>
                    You don’t have to know which second will become the
                    photograph.
                </p>

                <p>
                    That small difference can make the experience feel much less
                    like posing.
                </p>

                <h2>You can also ask someone else</h2>

                <p>
                    If another person is available, you don’t necessarily need
                    to turn them into a photographer.
                </p>

                <p>In fact, that can make things worse.</p>

                <p>“Stand there.”</p>

                <p>“Look at me.”</p>

                <p>“Smile.”</p>

                <p>“Do it again.”</p>

                <p>Now both people are thinking about the photograph.</p>

                <p>
                    Instead, the other person can simply hold the camera while
                    you continue doing something.
                </p>

                <p>Walk somewhere.</p>

                <p>Talk to them.</p>

                <p>Look at something nearby.</p>

                <p>Carry your coffee.</p>

                <p>Get into the car.</p>

                <p>
                    The goal isn’t to make the other person direct you into a
                    natural pose.
                </p>

                <p>It is to let them remove one job from your hands.</p>

                <p>You are still living the moment.</p>

                <p>They are just the one pressing the button.</p>

                <h2>The best method depends on the moment</h2>

                <p>
                    There isn’t one correct way to take a natural photo of
                    yourself.
                </p>

                <p>
                    A timer can work well when you are already doing something
                    that doesn’t require much movement.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Video can be useful when the moment involves more movement
                    or when you don’t want to think about the exact second the
                    photograph will happen.
                </p>

                <p>
                    A remote shutter can give you more control without making
                    you run back and forth to the phone.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Another person can make things easier when the scene
                    naturally involves conversation or movement.
                </p>

                <p>The important part is not the equipment.</p>

                <p>
                    It is the relationship between the activity and the camera.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If the camera controls the activity, the photograph will
                    often feel like a photograph being made.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If the activity continues and the camera simply records part
                    of it, the result has a better chance of feeling like life.
                </p>

                <h2>You don’t need to look unaware</h2>

                <p>There is another small trap here.</p>

                <p>
                    When people try to make a self-taken photo look candid, they
                    sometimes try very hard to look as though they don’t know
                    the camera is there.
                </p>

                <p>That can create another kind of performance.</p>

                <p>You don’t need to pretend the camera doesn’t exist.</p>

                <p>You can know it is there.</p>

                <p>
                    You just don’t want it to be the most important thing
                    happening.
                </p>

                <p>That is a much easier goal.</p>

                <p>
                    The difference is subtle, but you can usually feel it when
                    you see the finished photograph.
                </p>

                <p>One photo says:</p>

                <blockquote>
                    <p>I was trying to take a good picture.</p>
                </blockquote>

                <p>Another says:</p>

                <blockquote>
                    <p>
                        I happened to be doing something when this picture was
                        taken.
                    </p>
                </blockquote>

                <p>Neither one is necessarily technically better.</p>

                <p>But the second one often gives you more to look at.</p>

                <p>And more importantly, it gives you something to remember.</p>

                <h2>
                    Natural photos are easier when you stop asking the camera to
                    create the moment
                </h2>

                <p>
                    Taking your own photo will always involve some awareness of
                    the camera.
                </p>

                <p>That’s not a failure.</p>

                <p>
                    You don’t need to trick yourself into forgetting that you
                    are being photographed.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You just need to give yourself something more interesting to
                    pay attention to.
                </p>

                <p>The camera can be there.</p>

                <p>You can know it’s there.</p>

                <p>You can even press the button yourself.</p>

                <p>
                    The important thing is that the photograph doesn’t become
                    the whole moment.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Because when there is something happening before the camera
                    takes the picture, you have much less to perform.
                </p>

                <p>You simply have something to do.</p>
            </div>

            <ArticleFeedback
                article="how-to-take-natural-photos-of-yourself-when-nobody-is-behind-the-camera"
            />
        </article>
    </main>
</BaseLayout>

